From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Priestley <voicedrew11@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 18:38:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 12/14] vu1: cut interpreter overhead (~2.3x faster), bit-exact

The VU1 interpreter was ~90% of the frame in KFIV's 3D areas. Same
cycle and flag model, cheaper bookkeeping:

- Hazards: each decoded pair carries the ready-time slots it reads and
  writes (VF lanes, VI, ACC in one array), instead of re-deriving them
  from the usages with nested loops on every issue.
- VF/VI/ACC results are written back at issue. These registers are
  interlocked: every reader already stalls until the write's latency has
  elapsed, so the delayed write-back pipelines (and their copy-out /
  restore per instruction) could never be observed. A program still ends
  only once its last write would have landed (write-back horizon), so
  cycle counts are unchanged. Flags, Q, P and stores keep their pipelines.
- Pipelines track occupied slots in bitmasks and the earliest pending
  commit, so cycles with nothing due skip the commit scan, idle cycles are
  skipped, and commits still run in slot order.
- FMAC flags: a single-rounding (toward zero) result that is normal and
  below FLT_MAX can only report its sign, so the exact recomputation is
  skipped; ADD/SUB results that round to zero are exactly zero. Otherwise
  the exact result is computed in double, which classifies like the long
  double (a float product is exact in double); a sum that lands exactly on
  FLT_MAX still takes the long double. Operands are decoded once per
  instruction and normalized with SSE4.1 where available.
- XGKICK copies a qword at a time; decoded pairs are used by reference.

Verified by running the original interpreter in lockstep on every
microprogram of a KFIV session (720k runs into gameplay): registers,
flags, cycle counts, VU1 memory and XGKICK output identical.
